\section{Cost control}{

Two rails are on by default and are checked \strong{before} the first request:
\code{max_cost_usd} (5) and \code{max_calls} (400). Both are \code{\link[=gr_options]{gr_options()}}. A run that
trips one raises a classed error naming the option to change. \code{max_calls} is
re-checked before every subsequent call, so a run that hits it mid-flight
returns a \code{partial} answer rather than continuing to spend.

Preview segmentation for free before committing to a reader:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{doc <- gr_ingest("report.pdf")
gr_chunk_stats(gr_segment(doc, list(method = "sentence", max_tokens = 400)))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Diagnosing an answer}{

Every degradation is recorded, never silent. See \link{gr_answer} for the full
object, and \code{vignette}-free quick reference:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ans$partial     # did anything degrade?
ans$notes       # what: dropped_chunks, failed_calls, error, degraded_to_bm25
print(ans$trace)          # calls, tokens, first error
as_json(ans)              # every prompt and response from this one run
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Extending it}{

Each axis is a registry, so additions behave exactly like built-ins:
\code{\link[=gr_register_extractor]{gr_register_extractor()}}, \code{\link[=gr_register_cleaner]{gr_register_cleaner()}},
\code{\link[=gr_register_segmenter]{gr_register_segmenter()}}, \code{\link[=gr_register_reader]{gr_register_reader()}}, \code{\link[=gr_register_model]{gr_register_model()}}.
}

